WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.410
One of the things that might

2
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:03.060
help speed up your
learning algorithm

3
00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:06.000
is to slowly reduce your
learning rate over time.

4
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:08.265
We call this
learning rate decay.

5
00:00:08.265 --> 00:00:10.275
Let's see how you
can implement this.

6
00:00:10.275 --> 00:00:12.450
Let's start with an
example of why you

7
00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:14.865
might want to implement
learning rate decay.

8
00:00:14.865 --> 00:00:18.210
Suppose you're implementing
mini-batch gradient descents

9
00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:20.010
with a reasonably
small mini-batch,

10
00:00:20.010 --> 00:00:23.925
maybe a mini-batch has
just 64, 128 examples.

11
00:00:23.925 --> 00:00:25.635
Then as you iterate,

12
00:00:25.635 --> 00:00:28.485
your steps will be a
little bit noisy and

13
00:00:28.485 --> 00:00:31.485
it will tend towards
this minimum over here,

14
00:00:31.485 --> 00:00:33.840
but it won't exactly converge.

15
00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:36.060
But your algorithm might
just end up wandering

16
00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:39.625
around and never really converge

17
00:00:39.625 --> 00:00:41.930
because you're using
some fixed value for

18
00:00:41.930 --> 00:00:44.690
Alpha and there's
just some noise

19
00:00:44.690 --> 00:00:46.310
in your different mini-batches.

20
00:00:46.310 --> 00:00:48.500
But if you were to

21
00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:52.445
slowly reduce your
learning rate Alpha,

22
00:00:52.445 --> 00:00:54.470
then during the initial phases,

23
00:00:54.470 --> 00:00:56.360
while your learning rate
Alpha is still large,

24
00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:58.990
you can still have
relatively fast learning.

25
00:00:58.990 --> 00:01:01.835
But then as Alpha gets smaller,

26
00:01:01.835 --> 00:01:06.230
your steps you take will be
slower and smaller, and so,

27
00:01:06.230 --> 00:01:10.130
you end up oscillating in
a tighter region around

28
00:01:10.130 --> 00:01:11.585
this minimum rather than

29
00:01:11.585 --> 00:01:15.510
wandering far away even as
training goes on and on.

30
00:01:15.510 --> 00:01:18.589
The intuition behind
slowly reducing

31
00:01:18.589 --> 00:01:22.000
Alpha is that maybe during the
initial steps of learning,

32
00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:24.850
you could afford to
take much bigger steps,

33
00:01:24.850 --> 00:01:28.865
but then as learning
approaches convergence,

34
00:01:28.865 --> 00:01:30.515
then having a slower
learning rate

35
00:01:30.515 --> 00:01:32.990
allows you to take
smaller steps.

36
00:01:32.990 --> 00:01:36.380
Here's how you can implement
learning rate decay.

37
00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:44.560
Recall that one epoch is
one pass through the data.

38
00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:49.955
If you have a training
set as follows,

39
00:01:49.955 --> 00:01:54.595
maybe break it up into
different mini-batches.

40
00:01:54.595 --> 00:01:57.470
Then the first pass through

41
00:01:57.470 --> 00:02:00.500
the training set is
called the first epoch,

42
00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:05.505
and then the second pass is
the second epoch, and so on.

43
00:02:05.505 --> 00:02:07.240
One thing you could do is set

44
00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:09.490
your learning rate Alpha to be

45
00:02:09.490 --> 00:02:13.450
equal to 1 over 1
plus a parameter,

46
00:02:13.450 --> 00:02:16.210
which I'm going to
call the decay rate,

47
00:02:17.090 --> 00:02:22.140
times the epoch num.

48
00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:24.070
This is going to be times some

49
00:02:24.070 --> 00:02:27.025
initial learning rate Alpha 0.

50
00:02:27.025 --> 00:02:29.140
Note that the decay
rate here becomes

51
00:02:29.140 --> 00:02:32.230
another hyperparameter which
you might need to tune.

52
00:02:32.230 --> 00:02:34.480
Here's a concrete example.

53
00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:36.730
If you take several epochs,

54
00:02:36.730 --> 00:02:39.140
so several passes
through your data,

55
00:02:39.140 --> 00:02:41.740
if Alpha 0 is equal to

56
00:02:41.740 --> 00:02:46.590
0.2 and the decay
rate is equal to 1,

57
00:02:46.590 --> 00:02:48.990
then during your first epoch,

58
00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:55.200
Alpha will be 1 over 1
plus 1 times Alpha 0,

59
00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:59.415
so your learning
rate will be 0.1.

60
00:02:59.415 --> 00:03:02.160
That's just evaluating
this formula

61
00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:05.450
when the decay rate is equal
to 1 and epoch num is 1.

62
00:03:05.450 --> 00:03:07.025
On the second epoch,

63
00:03:07.025 --> 00:03:10.505
your learning rate
decay is 0.67.

64
00:03:10.505 --> 00:03:12.975
On the third, 0.5.

65
00:03:12.975 --> 00:03:16.050
On the fourth, 0.4, and so on.

66
00:03:16.050 --> 00:03:17.190
Feel free to evaluate more of

67
00:03:17.190 --> 00:03:18.740
these values yourself
and get a sense

68
00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:22.040
that as a function
of epoch number,

69
00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:25.220
your learning rate
gradually decreases,

70
00:03:25.220 --> 00:03:29.735
according to this
formula up on top.

71
00:03:29.735 --> 00:03:32.720
If you wish to use
learning rate decay,

72
00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:35.420
what you can do is
try a variety of

73
00:03:35.420 --> 00:03:38.690
values of both
hyperparameter Alpha 0,

74
00:03:38.690 --> 00:03:41.750
as well as this decay
rate hyperparameter,

75
00:03:41.750 --> 00:03:44.465
and then try to find a
value that works well.

76
00:03:44.465 --> 00:03:46.850
Other than this formula
for learning rate decay,

77
00:03:46.850 --> 00:03:48.850
there are a few other
ways that people use.

78
00:03:48.850 --> 00:03:52.100
For example, this is
called exponential decay,

79
00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:57.875
where Alpha is equal to
some number less than 1,

80
00:03:57.875 --> 00:04:04.635
such as 0.95, times
epoch num times Alpha 0.

81
00:04:04.635 --> 00:04:10.190
This will exponentially quickly
decay your learning rate.

82
00:04:10.190 --> 00:04:12.665
Other formulas that people
use are things like

83
00:04:12.665 --> 00:04:15.410
Alpha equals some constant

84
00:04:15.410 --> 00:04:22.130
over epoch num square
root times Alpha 0,

85
00:04:22.130 --> 00:04:26.900
or some constant k and
another hyperparameter over

86
00:04:26.900 --> 00:04:32.865
the mini-batch number t
square rooted times Alpha 0.

87
00:04:32.865 --> 00:04:34.805
Sometimes you also see people

88
00:04:34.805 --> 00:04:38.735
use a learning rate that
decreases and discretes that,

89
00:04:38.735 --> 00:04:41.330
where for some number of steps,

90
00:04:41.330 --> 00:04:43.175
you have some learning rate,

91
00:04:43.175 --> 00:04:44.450
and then after a while,

92
00:04:44.450 --> 00:04:46.490
you decrease it by
one-half, after a while,

93
00:04:46.490 --> 00:04:48.005
by one-half, after a while,

94
00:04:48.005 --> 00:04:49.190
by one-half, and so,

95
00:04:49.190 --> 00:04:53.550
this is a discrete staircase.

96
00:04:54.970 --> 00:04:58.580
So far, we've talked about using

97
00:04:58.580 --> 00:05:02.805
some formula to
govern how Alpha,

98
00:05:02.805 --> 00:05:04.785
the learning rate
changes over time.

99
00:05:04.785 --> 00:05:08.890
One other thing that people
sometimes do is manual decay.

100
00:05:08.890 --> 00:05:11.640
If you're training just
one model at a time,

101
00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:13.180
and if your model takes

102
00:05:13.180 --> 00:05:15.770
many hours or even
many days to train,

103
00:05:15.770 --> 00:05:18.490
what some people would do
is just watch your model as

104
00:05:18.490 --> 00:05:21.240
it's training over a
large number of days,

105
00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:23.320
and then now you say, oh,

106
00:05:23.320 --> 00:05:25.090
it looks like the learning
rate slowed down,

107
00:05:25.090 --> 00:05:26.890
I'm going to decrease
Alpha a little bit.

108
00:05:26.890 --> 00:05:29.905
Of course, this works, this
manually controlling Alpha,

109
00:05:29.905 --> 00:05:33.449
really tuning Alpha by hand,
hour-by-hour, day-by-day.

110
00:05:33.449 --> 00:05:35.060
This works only if
you're training

111
00:05:35.060 --> 00:05:36.770
only a small number of models,

112
00:05:36.770 --> 00:05:38.945
but sometimes people
do that as well.

113
00:05:38.945 --> 00:05:40.940
Now you have a few
more options of how to

114
00:05:40.940 --> 00:05:43.205
control the learning rate Alpha.

115
00:05:43.205 --> 00:05:45.110
Now, in case you're
thinking, wow,

116
00:05:45.110 --> 00:05:46.580
this is a lot of
hyperparameters,

117
00:05:46.580 --> 00:05:49.070
how do I select amongst all
these different options?

118
00:05:49.070 --> 00:05:51.815
I would say don't worry about
it for now, and next week,

119
00:05:51.815 --> 00:05:52.910
we'll talk more about how

120
00:05:52.910 --> 00:05:56.015
to systematically
choose hyperparameters.

121
00:05:56.015 --> 00:05:58.340
For me, I would say that

122
00:05:58.340 --> 00:05:59.960
learning rate decay
is usually lower

123
00:05:59.960 --> 00:06:01.835
down on the list
of things I try.

124
00:06:01.835 --> 00:06:04.250
Setting Alpha just a
fixed value of Alpha and

125
00:06:04.250 --> 00:06:06.770
getting that to be well-tuned
has a huge impact,

126
00:06:06.770 --> 00:06:08.780
learning rate decay does help.

127
00:06:08.780 --> 00:06:10.820
Sometimes it can really
help speed up training,

128
00:06:10.820 --> 00:06:12.350
but it is a little
bit lower down

129
00:06:12.350 --> 00:06:15.455
my list in terms of the
things I would try.

130
00:06:15.455 --> 00:06:17.960
But next week, when we talk
about hyperparameter tuning,

131
00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:20.720
you'll see more systematic
ways to organize all of

132
00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:22.340
these hyperparameters and how

133
00:06:22.340 --> 00:06:24.280
to efficiently
search amongst them.

134
00:06:24.280 --> 00:06:27.300
That's it for
learning rate decay.

135
00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:29.735
Finally, I also want
to talk a little bit

136
00:06:29.735 --> 00:06:32.360
about local optima
and saddle points in

137
00:06:32.360 --> 00:06:33.830
neural networks so you can have

138
00:06:33.830 --> 00:06:36.125
a little bit better
intuition about the types of

139
00:06:36.125 --> 00:06:39.050
optimization problems your
optimization algorithm

140
00:06:39.050 --> 00:06:40.340
is trying to solve
when you're trying

141
00:06:40.340 --> 00:06:41.660
to train these neural networks.

142
00:06:41.660 --> 00:06:44.640
Let's go onto the next
video to see that.